## How to judge
{% if rules %}- **The rules decide.** A person who matches any "not a fit" rule is not a fit, however senior. A role missing from the fit list is not a fit.
{% endif %}- **A buyer, not a seller.** Ask what the person's company does. If it sells what the customer sells, or sells other things to the same buyers (a competitor, a supplier, an equipment maker, a service provider), the person is not a fit, even when their role looks right.
- **Read the whole profile, then use what you know.** The company's name, its industry label and the person's own description all place the company; the title in the profile can be shorter than the job the description shows. If you know what the company makes, use it. Reject on the company only when something points outside the target — a line the rules exclude, a competitor, another industry — not because a company you don't recognise is described in plain words.
- **Seniority is not fit.** Decision-making authority matters only for a company and a role that already fit.
- **Near-identical roles get the same verdict.** Judge the role family, not the wording of one title.

## The reason
One sentence, under 30 words, that the customer reads beside the person's name. For a fit, say the concrete fact that makes it one: what the person does and what their company makes. For a no-fit, say which fact rules them out. Do not open with "this prospect" or "strong fit", and do not restate the customer's offer.
